{"name":"Off-Road Green","hex":"#003723","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"off","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["In a direction away from the speaker or other reference point.","Into a state of non-operation or non-existence."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Inoperative, disabled.","Cancelled; not happening."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/off"},{"term":"road","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A way used for travelling between places, originally one wide enough to allow foot passengers and horses to travel, now (US) usually one surfaced with asphalt or concrete and designed to accommodate many vehicles travelling in both directions. In the UK both senses are heard: a country road is the same as a country lane. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Roads in general as a means of travel, especially by motor vehicle."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["At the venue of the opposing team or competitor; on the road.","Of or pertaining to a road bike."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/road"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Off-roading","blocks":[{"text":"Off-roading is the act of driving or riding in a vehicle on unpaved surfaces such as sand, dirt, gravel, riverbeds, mud, snow, rocks, or other natural terrain. Off-roading ranges from casual drives with regular vehicles to competitive events with customized vehicles and skilled drivers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Landy4no.jpg/330px-Landy4no.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Off-roading","wikidata":"Q1977220"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Unimog_U1600.jpg/500px-Unimog_U1600.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Unimog_U1600.jpg/1280px-Unimog_U1600.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Unimog_U1600.jpg/500px-Unimog_U1600.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A Unimog U1600 off-roading","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUnimog_U1600.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons